# p_8e4d — Home-based couple with two work zones; object_7 lives in the living room, object_14 stays at the desk (fork of p_c4e9)

Fork of p_c4e9, triggered by the catastrophic failure of its two distinguishing claims: "object_14 at receptacle_19, weekday 13-18h" (0 for, 5 against) and "object_7 at receptacle_19, weekday 9-17h" (0 for, 4 against). The per-object evidence is unambiguous: object_7 has 0/4 weekday 9-17h looks at receptacle_19 finding it, and object_14 has 2/4 looks at receptacle_12 finding it (versus 0/5 at receptacle_19 in the afternoon window). The parent's core thesis—that objects rotate FROM the desk TO the side shelf in the afternoon—is contradicted by the data.

Change 1: object_7 is NOT at receptacle_19 during weekday work hours. The worst-objects list shows it at receptacle_10 (living room) at 18:00 on day 2. I move object_7 to receptacle_10 during weekday 9-17h and keep it at receptacle_19 in the evening and on weekends. This makes sense if object_7 is a decorative or reference item that gets pulled into the living room for the workday (perhaps as a backdrop or secondary reference) and returned to its shelf at night.

Change 2: object_14 stays at receptacle_12 all day. I remove the 13-18h receptacle_19 block entirely. The parent's "two work zones" thesis is weakened: receptacle_19 is a storage/shelf area, not an active afternoon workspace. object_14 (laptop/tablet) is a desk item that stays put.

Change 3: I keep the kitchen split (object_4 at receptacle_18, object_15/16 at receptacle_4) and the "nothing leaves the house" claim, which are well-supported (object_4: 5 for, 0 against; object_2: 8 for, 1 against).

What sets this apart from p_c4e9: object_7 is at receptacle_10 (not receptacle_19) during weekday work hours, and object_14 never moves to receptacle_19. What would refute it: object_7 found at receptacle_19 during a weekday 10:00-16:00 look, or object_14 found at receptacle_19 during a weekday 14:00-17:00 look.

